# Session hygiene — context and cost

## Orientation (start of multi-step or unfamiliar work)

- Prefer **`docs/library/REPO_DIGEST.md`** for a quick project inventory and doc anchors before globbing or opening many top-level files.
- Use **`@path`** or **`docs/library/ASSESSMENT_INPUTS.md`** when the user asks for assessments; attach **`@Assessment-Scope-V1_1`** for scoring rules (not always injected).

## Reads and search

- Do **not** re-read an entire file wholesale if its contents are already in this conversation unless the user asks, something changed on disk, or you need lines you never opened.
- Prefer **scoped** reads (`Read` with offsets, `Grep`, `SemanticSearch`) over “list everything.”
- If the user **attached** a path with **`@`** in Cursor, treat the **attachment / summary as already in context** — do not issue a redundant full-file `Read` for that path unless you need specific line ranges that are not in the thread.
- **Assessment passes:** after reading `docs/assessments/LATEST.md` (or `ASSESSMENT_INPUTS.md`), **cite by section / improvement number** rather than pasting large verbatim blocks into the reply on every follow-up.

## Batch work

- For a list of related tasks: treat the **first user message** as the shared preamble (scope, pointers, numbered list); work **one item at a time** unless the user says to parallelize against repo policy (`Agent-Execution-Policy.mdc`).

## When to suggest a new chat

If the thread is **very long** (many substantive turns / huge prior tool output) **or** the user pivots to a **different subsystem** than the bulk of earlier context, briefly suggest **starting a fresh chat** so the next task is not billed on duplicated megatokens—unless they explicitly want this thread to continue.
